# Local dev default: a plain file on disk, no setup needed. For a real
# deploy (e.g. Vercel, or any serverless/ephemeral-filesystem host), point
# this at a hosted Turso database instead — libSQL (what Turso runs) speaks
# the same protocol either way, so no other code changes: `libsql://<db
# name>-<org>.turso.io` — and set DATABASE_AUTH_TOKEN below. Get both from
# `turso db show <db>` / `turso db tokens create <db>` (or the Turso
# dashboard) after `turso db create <db>`.
DATABASE_URL="file:./prisma/dev.db"

# Required only when DATABASE_URL is a libsql:// URL (a local file: URL
# needs no auth). Leave unset for local dev.
DATABASE_AUTH_TOKEN=""

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-..."

# The accuracy review (ACC2): after a pack is generated, a second model pass
# checks every question and fixes or drops the wrong ones before the pack is
# saved. On unless this is exactly "off" (any case). Off, packs are saved
# unreviewed and marked not checked, and the host sees a banner saying so.
# It is the switch for an incident — a slow or failing review, or its cost —
# and it never affects generation itself. Read on every request, but Vercel
# only applies a changed variable to new deployments, so redeploy after
# changing it. See src/lib/review-pack.ts.
PACK_REVIEW=""

# Optional override for the free-tier ceiling (packs per rolling 30 days per
# creator cookie). Unset means 2, which is what production runs; raise it on
# a preview deploy to exercise the wizard without burning the real allowance.
# Read once at startup, so a change needs a redeploy. A blank or malformed
# value falls back to 2 rather than locking everyone out. Note this is a
# separate ceiling from the per-IP limiter on POST /api/packs/generate
# (5 per 10 minutes), which this does not affect.
FREE_PACK_LIMIT=""

# Hard ceilings on how many packs the WHOLE deployment generates per UTC day,
# checked before the model is called. These are what actually bound the
# Anthropic bill: the per-creator FREE_PACK_LIMIT above is counted against a
# cookie, and a request that sends no cookie is handed a fresh allowance, so
# it caps a polite visitor and nothing else. These have no identity in the
# key, so rotating or dropping cookies does not move them.
#
# Defaults are 20 (free) and 50 (pro) if unset or malformed, chosen against
# the owner's API budget rather than against expected demand: at ACC5's measured
# $0.075 a pack ($0.136 for five rounds of ten; Opus 5.5 generating and
# checking), the two together are about $5-9.50 a day; about $100 if every call
# ran to its 16k max_tokens. Free and Pro count in separate buckets,
# so free traffic can never exhaust a paying customer's capacity, and the two
# ceilings therefore ADD UP — budget against their sum, not either one. Pro has
# no per-user cap, which is what keeps "as many quiz packs as you want" on
# /pricing true; its ceiling is a runaway-loop backstop, not a product limit.
# An explicit "0" is a kill switch for that plan.
#
# Read per request, so changing the value needs no code change or rebuild.
#
# IMPORTANT: these are only truly global when UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL/TOKEN are
# set below. Without Upstash each serverless instance keeps its own counter,
# so the ceiling is really N ceilings and bounds the bill N times less
# tightly. Set Upstash in production.
FREE_DAILY_PACK_CEILING=""
PRO_DAILY_PACK_CEILING=""

# --- The per-caller limits added in the launch batch ---------------------------
#
# All five are optional: each has a safe default in code and the app is correct
# with none of them set. All are Upstash-backed, so the same caveat as the
# ceilings above applies — without UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL/TOKEN each instance
# counts on its own. An explicit "0" is a kill switch for any of them, and
# anything that is not a non-negative integer falls back to the default rather
# than propagating a typo.

# How many packs one Pro subscriber may generate in a UTC day. Default 10.
# /pricing and /terms print this number from PRO_DAILY_PACK_ALLOWANCE in
# src/lib/pricing.ts, and src/lib/pricing.test.ts fails if the two disagree — so
# if you change this, change that (H2).
PRO_USER_DAILY_PACK_LIMIT=""

# Pro fair use (PRC5): packs one Pro account may generate in a rolling 30 days.
# Default 40; 0 switches the cap off. Comped accounts count. Unpublished — no
# page or message prints it; a capped host is told only when it resets.
PRO_USER_PERIOD_PACK_LIMIT=""
# The same cap while the subscription is "trialing". Default 10; 0 switches it off.
PRO_TRIAL_PACK_LIMIT=""

# How many free packs one IP address may generate in a UTC day. Default 5 —
# deliberately not 1, because a pub, an office or a campus is one address
# carrying several genuine hosts. FREE only; Pro subscribers are exempt (H1a).
FREE_IP_DAILY_LIMIT=""

# Sign-in emails. Resend's free plan allows 100 a day and that allowance is
# shared with another product on the same account, so these bound what the
# internet can make us send. Defaults: 3 per address per hour, 60 a day in
# total — short of 100 on purpose (M2).
SIGNIN_CODE_PER_ADDRESS_HOURLY=""
SIGNIN_CODE_DAILY_LIMIT=""

# Extra Paddle price ids that should still grant Pro, comma-separated. The
# checkout's own NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_PRICE_MONTHLY/ANNUAL are always recognised;
# this is for a price retired from the checkout that still has subscribers on it,
# who must keep their Pro. Anything matching no known price is another product's
# and is acknowledged and ignored (C2).
PADDLE_EXTRA_PRICE_IDS=""

# Optional operator override for DELETE /api/packs/[id], supplied in an
# x-admin-token header. It only ever *adds* the ability to delete any pack:
# with this unset there is no operator, so the route accepts nobody but the
# pack's own creator cookie. Safe to leave empty — a deploy that forgets it
# loses the override, not the protection.
ADMIN_TOKEN=""

# Optional, both required together. Backs the per-IP rate limiter
# (src/lib/rate-limit.ts) with Upstash Redis instead of an in-process Map,
# so limits are actually shared/enforced across serverless instances rather
# than resetting per cold start. Leave unset for solo local dev — the
# limiter falls back to the in-memory store automatically. Create a
# database at https://console.upstash.com and copy its REST URL/token.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=""
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=""

# Optional. How long one Upstash command may take before the shared counter
# treats the store as unreachable, in milliseconds. Default 500.
#
# An unreachable Upstash no longer takes the site down (the limiter fails open,
# the daily ceiling fails closed), but with the client's default retry budget it
# made the site slow instead: 4.34s for a request consulting one counter and
# 8.67s for two, measured against a host that would not answer. One retry and a
# 500ms deadline bring the worst case to about a second.
#
# Raise it if you see "ratelimit-redis-error" in the logs without an actual
# outage — that means a healthy-but-slow store is being written off, which costs
# abuse protection on the limiter and refuses generation on the ceiling. Do not
# lower it much: too short is the same as no store at all.
UPSTASH_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS=""

# Paddle Billing: Pro subscriptions. Sandbox values locally and on preview
# deployments, live values in production.
#
# The four NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are publishable (they end up in the browser
# bundle) and are read at BUILD time: set them in Vercel as plain (non-secret)
# variables, and redeploy after changing them. A production build fails if any
# is empty — see next.config.ts. The prices must be Paddle price ids (pri_…)
# for exactly the amounts in src/lib/pricing.ts.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_ENV="sandbox"
NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_CLIENT_TOKEN=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_PRICE_MONTHLY=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_PADDLE_PRICE_ANNUAL=""
# Server-side secrets, read at request time. PADDLE_API_KEY opens the customer
# portal (Manage subscription); PADDLE_NOTIFICATION_WEBHOOK_SECRET verifies
# every call to /api/paddle/webhook and must be the secret of the notification
# destination that points at this deployment. Mark both sensitive.
# The checkout's customData is signed with a key derived from
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET (src/lib/paddle/checkout-token.ts), so there is no
# separate signing secret to set.
PADDLE_API_KEY=""
PADDLE_NOTIFICATION_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host accounts (Better Auth)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hosts sign in with Google or an emailed code; teams never sign in. See
# src/lib/auth.ts. Everything in this block is REQUIRED in production except
# where noted — src/lib/auth.ts throws rather than booting with a development
# fallback secret.

# Signs the session cookie. Generate one with `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# Changing it signs every existing host out, which is the correct response to
# a leak and a nuisance otherwise. Production refuses to start without it.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=""

# The origin this deployment answers on, no trailing slash: the OAuth
# redirect and the sign-in link are both built from it, so a wrong value sends
# people to a domain that cannot sign them in. Production:
# https://triviafoundry.com. Locally, http://localhost:3000 (or whatever port
# `next dev` printed). On a Vercel preview, leave it UNSET: the origin is
# resolved from the request and bounded by an allowed-host list that covers
# the production domain and *.vercel.app.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=""

# Google sign-in. Both or neither: with either missing there is simply no
# Google button and the email code is the only way in (see
# isGoogleSignInConfigured). Create an OAuth 2.0 Web application client at
# https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials and register the exact
# redirect URI(s):
#   https://triviafoundry.com/api/auth/callback/google
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google   (local dev only)
# Preview deployments get a new hostname on every push and cannot be
# registered, so Google sign-in does not work on a preview by design — use
# the email code there.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Sends the sign-in email — a six-digit code and a link carrying the same
# code — over Resend's HTTP API (no SDK). Get a key at
# https://resend.com/api-keys and verify the sending domain first, or Resend
# rejects the send with a 403.
#
# In production a missing key is a hard failure: the alternative is telling
# people to check an inbox for mail that was never sent. Outside production a
# missing key logs the code to the server console instead, which is what
# makes `npm run dev` work with no mail provider at all.
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# The From address on that email, e.g. "TriviaFoundry <quiz@triviafoundry.com>".
# Must be on a domain verified in Resend. Required whenever RESEND_API_KEY is set.
EMAIL_FROM=""

# TEST ENVIRONMENTS ONLY. Captures sign-in emails in memory instead of mailing
# them, and opens GET /api/test/sign-in-emails to read them back — which is how
# the e2e suite signs in, since a browser cannot open an email.
#
# It cannot be switched on in production: the gate needs NODE_ENV !==
# "production" AND this set to exactly "1" AND no RESEND_API_KEY, and the
# first of those is unsatisfiable on a production deploy. Do not set it here.
# See src/lib/sign-in-email.ts and its tests.
SIGN_IN_EMAIL_CAPTURE=""

# Vercel Cron's credential. Vercel sends `Authorization: Bearer <this>` with
# each scheduled call in vercel.json (the daily retention sweep at
# /api/cron/retention), and the route refuses anything else. Unset, the route
# refuses everyone — including the scheduler — so the sweep simply does not
# run. Any long random string; set it for Production only (Vercel does not
# run crons on previews). See src/lib/cron-auth.ts.
CRON_SECRET=""
